GREETER(1)                  User Commands

NAME
    greeter - the graphical login

SYNOPSIS
    greeter [-s]

DESCRIPTION
    init runs the greeter as root on the console, as the console entry of
    /etc/init.conf names it:

        console greeter greeter

    On a machine without a display, or when the display server or the
    login window cannot start, the greeter runs login(1) in its place.
    Replacing greeter with login in the console entry gives the console
    login alone.

    The greeter starts the display server and restarts it when it ends.
    Its window lists root and the accounts from uid 1000 on, with the
    account after root selected, and asks for the password, which is not
    shown. A wrong password is refused with a pause. An account without a
    password, such as user on a new system, logs in with an empty one and
    then has to choose a new password, typed twice in a second window,
    before its session starts. Restart and Shut down ask init to restart
    or power off the machine.

    After a login the greeter admits the account to the display server,
    tells init that the session on the console belongs to it, which lets
    the user power off with initctl(1), and runs startgui -s as the
    account in its home with a fresh environment. Log out in the panel's
    menu ends the session. The greeter then ends the programs the session
    left behind, withdraws the admission and shows its window again.

    -s mirrors the log of the display server to the console, as the boot
    tests read it.

SEE ALSO
    login(1), su(1), sudo(1), initctl(1), init.conf(5), index(7)
